  "action": "take_coach",
  "detail": "I push the blanket back. Sit up. The radio is still doing its three bright notes. I reach over and turn the dial to static, then off. Feet on the cold tile. I pull the flannel shirt on, the work pants, the boots I slept in. Coat over. I open the bag on the chair, find the coach ticket in the side pocket, smooth it flat, tuck it in the breast pocket where it'll sit against my chest all the way to Pittsburgh. I don't look at the envelope on the desk. I don't look at the piano. I don't look at the window where the van is still backfiring. I walk to the lobby, sit at the counter, put a dime in the machine, drink the coffee standing up. Then I ask the desk clerk the coach departure time. I write it down on the back of a matchbook. 2:15 PM. I have the afternoon. I sit on the bench by the coat check. I do not open the envelope. I do not go to the ballroom. I do not go to the Rialto. I do not help the driver. I sit. I wait. At 1:45 I am at the door. I board. I sit by the window. I watch the town go. I watch the highway go. I watch the miles go. I get off in Pittsburgh. I call the terminal number I already know. I say: \"Hi. Is the van driver still confirming for 8 AM tomorrow?\" I listen. I say: \"Six-forty. Gloves are in my bag. Blankets rolled. You don't let him sit in the driveway.\" I hang up. I find a bench. I sleep. I wake at six. I am at her door at 7:15. The couch is a couch. It is in Pittsburgh. I am in Pittsburgh. That is the whole forecast.",
  "intention": "Be in Pittsburgh. Move the couch. Go home. Nothing else is on the board.","words": "\"Tuesday.\" (said to no one, to the radio, to the room. One word. Then silence. Then the coin in the machine. Then the matchbook. Then the bench. Then the highway. Then Pittsburgh. Then the couch.)"
}

